ion errors.\n\nThe inventory service is checking for a status field in the cancelled event that may not always be present, but the test explicitly includes it, so the real blocker is the EventBus handler issue preventing the event from reaching inventory in the first place.\n\nHowever, looking at the actual `cancelOrder` implementation, it doesn't emit the status field at all, which means the handler's early return check would prevent stock from being released even if the EventBus issue were fixed. To make the orders test pass, I need to either add the status field to the emitted event or remove that check from the handler.\n\nNow I'm spotting another issue in the BillingService - the `handleRefund` method isn't bound to the service instance when registered as an event listener, unlike the `handleStockReserved` handler which explicitly uses `.bind(this)`. This means when the callback fires, `this` will be undefined inside the method.\n\nFor the billing calculator, there's a rounding precision problem where individual rounding of discount and tax amounts doesn't guarantee the final total rounds correctly to two decimal places - the test expects 130.86 but gets 130.87 because the sum of separately rounded values doesn't equal the rounded sum. I see the issue now—the test expects the discount to be applied to the full subtotal before rounding, then the tax calculated on that intermediate value, rather than rounding after each step. So I need to use 118.966 (the unrounded after-discount amount) for the tax calculation to get 11.8966, which rounds to 11.90, and then the total becomes 130.8626, rounding down to 130.86. Rewriting the calculation function... Verifying the calculations against the test cases to make sure the rounding works correctly across different scenarios.\n\nNow I'm spotting a bug in the refund logic where the condition should use strict greater-than instead of greater-than-or-equal-to when checking against the refund window threshold. I see the real issue now—the test is actually failing because the refund window check is using `>=` when it should be `>`, so a refund on exactly day 30 throws an error before it even gets to the point where `refunded?.status` would be undefined. The condition needs to allow day 30 as a valid refund day. The test expects refund to succeed on day 30, but it's currently failing because the boundary check uses `>=` instead of `>`. There's also likely an issue with the repository update not preserving existing order fields like status and items, which would cause the refund to fail even if the timing check passes. So the real culprit is that repository update bug wiping out the status field—when cancelOrder tries to check if the order is 'confirmed', it finds undefined instead, returns undefined, and then refundOrder does the same thing with 'cancelled', leaving the test with an undefined result instead of the expected 'refunded' status. I need to fix that update bug first before tackling the refund window logic.\n\nNow looking at the \"rejects refund after 30-day window\" test to see how it validates that orders older than 30 days can't be refunded...\n\nThe test is currently failing due to the EventBus handler leak, but once that's fixed, I need to trace through what happens: the order gets created, backdated to 31 days ago, then cancelled. The real issue is whether the refund window check will properly reject it—but that depends on Bug 2 being fixed so the createdAt timestamp actually persists through the update.\n\nBoth the `>= 30` and `> 30` conditions would reject a 31-day-old order, so the difference only matters at exactly 30 days. Let me dig deeper into Bug 6 to understand what's actually going wrong.\n\nWhen `createdAt` is set to `Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs`, the subsequent check calculates the elapsed time as roughly 30 days due to the tiny delay between setting and checking. Math.floor rounds this down to 30, so with `>= 30` the refund gets rejected, but with `> 30` it would be allowed. This is the crux of the bug—the boundary condition at exactly 30 days behaves differently depending on which operator is used.\n\nNow looking at the summary of all the bugs I've identified: the EventBus accumulates handlers across test instances because they're stored at the module level, the OrderRepository.update() method loses existing fields by not spreading them in, the InventoryService has an inverted guard that prevents cancelled orders from being handled properly, and this refund window comparison uses the wrong operator.\n\nI'm also noticing the BillingService handler isn't bound to the correct context, the total calculation rounds intermediate values when it should only round the final result, and I need to double-check my understanding of Bug 3 since the cancellation event doesn't actually include a status field in the payload.\n\nLooking at the event emission in OrderService, it sends orderId, reason, items, and timestamp but no status property. That means the guard checking `eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled'` will always be true and cause an early return, preventing stock from being released. The cleanest fix is to remove that guard entirely since the handler only listens to CANCELLED events anyway, so we know it's a cancellation by definition. the status check from the handler since the event type itself signals cancellation, making the fix cleaner and allowing both tests to pass without requiring the service to emit a status field it doesn't currently include.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2503,"cache_read_input_tokens":38644,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2503},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"81f81a52-5619-4aef-926d-8bbc6ae12830","uuid":"3e8d4414-0445-44c4-81